      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "(c) 2006 Wiley-Liss, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": "Percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) of heavily calcified vessels poses several problems including difficulty in delivering stents to the target lesion. Effective strategies include rotational atherectomy and the use of a \"buddy\" wire, the latter acting as a track that directs the stent away from the vessel wall. There are no reports in the literature of using a second \"buddy\" wire when one fails. We report on a case of a second \"buddy\" wire saving the day during PCI of a highly calcified right coronary artery wherein the stent failed to reach the lesion until the second wire was deployed."
      },
